artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a large flammable forest or wildland landscape on which fuel treatments (e.g., prescribed burns, mechanical thinning, fuel breaks) can be applied to alter the spatial and temporal behavior of wildfire spread and the effectiveness of suppression efforts. Fire propagation depends, in a dynamic and stochastic manner, on the spatial distribution of fuels, weather conditions, topography, ignition locations, and suppression actions taken once fires occur.

Formalize and solve the following planning problem:

Let L be a landscape partitioned into a finite set of management units. For each unit i and each planning period t in a discrete horizon, let x_{i,t} ∈ {0,1} indicate whether a fuel treatment is applied at unit i in period t, subject to budget, capacity, ecological, and adjacency constraints. Treatments alter local fuel state and thereby modify the (random) fire-spread dynamics, which depend jointly on weather scenarios, ignition processes, and suppression policies.

Develop a tractable optimization model (and accompanying algorithm) that determines an implementation schedule {x_{i,t}} maximizing the expected reduction in wildfire damage (or equivalently minimizing expected loss, area burned, or values-at-risk) over the planning horizon, while explicitly accounting for:
(a) the stochastic dynamics of fire ignition, spread, and weather across the landscape;
(b) the interaction between fuel treatments and suppression effectiveness;
(c) the spatial scale of large landscapes (tens of thousands to millions of units);
(d) the temporal dimension of when treatments should be applied and reapplied.

Provide a formulation whose size and computational complexity remain manageable on landscape-scale instances, and establish guarantees (exact, approximation, or empirical) on the quality of the resulting treatment schedules.

